<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>Science | dw.com</title><subtitle type="html">Science</subtitle><id>https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss_en_science</id><updated>2026-11-17T17:42:00Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss_en_science" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.dw.com/s-12526?maca=en-rss_en_science-22531-xml-mrss" /><link rel="via" href="https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss_en_science" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>NASA's Voyager 1 reaches a light-day away from Earth</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:4d7111f0084a2980cd6df105f74a225c7cc31a518d5f03d31467a89a20101479</id><updated>2026-11-17T17:42:00Z</updated><published>2026-11-16T17:24:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/nasa-s-voyager-1-reaches-a-light-day-away-from-earth/a-79961817--190e9087abf2.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/nasa-s-voyager-1-reaches-a-light-day-away-from-earth/a-79961817?maca=en-rss_en_science-22531-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">When just saying 'hello' takes a two-day round trip, you're a light-day, or 26 billion kilometers, from Earth. Only one human-made object has ever traveled that far — and its journey into interstellar space continues.</summary><category term="Science" /></entry><entry><title>Intelligent women get less help at work, study finds</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:503651a616710a7a00054a9959391138b455017b41dc65dea45a5eb35f2cc792</id><updated>2026-11-17T17:42:00Z</updated><published>2026-11-12T07:21:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/intelligent-women-get-less-help-at-work-study-finds/a-79910881--d3f9551f1647.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/intelligent-women-get-less-help-at-work-study-finds/a-79910881?maca=en-rss_en_science-22531-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Women who scored high on intelligence tests were seen as more hostile by their teammates and got less help, a new study finds. High-scoring men faced no such penalty. Research explains why.</summary><category term="Science" /></entry><entry><title>DW Science explainer on the default mode network: what your brain does when you aren't focusing on anything, and why it's important.</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:824b026590700e325fb649d1828038342ddee4b4e829c4aca974242526f9bad7</id><updated>2026-11-17T17:42:00Z</updated><published>2026-11-11T06:57:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/dw-science-explainer-on-the-default-mode-network-what-your-brain-does-when-you-aren-t-focusing-on-an/a-79908787--cf91bcf53b1d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/dw-science-explainer-on-the-default-mode-network-what-your-brain-does-when-you-aren-t-focusing-on-anything-and-why-it-s-important/a-79908787?maca=en-rss_en_science-22531-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Doing nothing might be the ultimate brain hack. When you're bored, the brain goes into default mode network. It's not only healthy for you, but it may also shape the very essence of your identity.</summary><category term="Science" /></entry></feed>
